# GitHub Pull Request #394

## Title
feat(renderers): show [skill] lines for returned skill reads

## Description
## Summary

Vanilla Pi shows `[skill] <name>` when its read tool loads a skill file, so it's easy to see when the agent uses a skill. Pit's `typescript` results showed only `Read <path>`. This PR adds the same marker, determined from what the program returned.

<img width="1044" height="368" alt="Herdr 2026-11-16 at 11 59 29@2x" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/59e149cd-6d3f-4137-aa0e-492aa112a70a" />

- **Returned skill read:** the headline names the skill instead of the path, e.g. `✓ [skill] pit-delivery, 1-176 of 176, raw`.
- **Skill reads inside a returned batch, array or object:** one row per skill after the headline, with `⚠ partial: lines …` when its reads miss lines of the skill file, and `+ references/guide.md` for other files read from the skill's folder.
- **Skill references:** a returned read of another file in a skill's folder is shown as `[skill ref] <name>`.

### How skills are identified

1. On each prompt, Pit stores the skills Pi loaded (`{ name, filePath }`) in its session state.
2. When a call finishes, `buildToolResult` searches the returned (already size-limited) value for read results and matches their files against those skills. Matches are saved as `details.skillFiles`: the skill's own file, or the innermost skill folder containing the file. A skill file outside a `SKILL.md` folder owns no folder.
3. The read renderers use `skillFiles`. Without it (older sessions), any `SKILL.md` is marked, named after its folder, as Pi's own read renderer does.

The check runs once per call; drawing reads only saved data, so saved sessions show the skills they were run with.

### Not marked

Reads the program didn't return, and reads outside the workspace API, e.g. `cat` through `shell.exec`.

### Test tooling

- `ux.manageSession` gains a `skills` option (passes Pi's `--skill`).
- New `skill`, `skill-return` and `skill-ref` offline fixtures, listed in the tmux guide.

## Validation

- [x] Targeted tests pass (`test/tool/skill-reads.test.ts`)
- [x] `npm run check` passes
- [x] `npm test` passes
- [x] `npm run coverage` passes
- [x] `npm run package:check` passes
- [x] Documentation is updated or not required (pit-terminal-ux tmux guide)
- [x] Interactive `/reload` smoke testing is complete or not required: in a real Pi (`pi -e .` at `03e87f0`, normal skills and model), a returned skill read showed `✓ [skill] test-driven-development, 1-116 of 116, raw`; an object with a reference read and a batch showed `[skill ref] pit-terminal-ux + references/tmux.md`, `[skill] pit-delivery ⚠ partial: lines 1-10 of 194` and `[skill] pit-test-audit`; after `/reload`, a returned reference read showed `⚠ [skill ref] pit-terminal-ux, references/tmux.md, 1-3 of 111, hashed, more available` and a full read `✓ [skill] pit-delivery, 1-194 of 194, raw`, and the earlier results replayed with their skill rows.
- [x] No secrets, credentials, private paths, or unsanitized session data are included

## Risk

Display only: what the model receives is unchanged. Results gain an optional `details.skillFiles`; older sessions without it still render, using the `SKILL.md` rule. `renderResultValue`'s fourth parameter changes from a `details` boolean to an options object `{ details, skillFiles }`; its one in-repo caller and one test are updated. To roll back, revert the two commits.


## Task
let's ship a new patch release

## Your Reply

pi-action posts your final response as a comment on this issue or pull request. **Do not post your reply yourself**, for example with `gh issue comment`, `gh pr comment`, `gh pr review`, or `gh api`. That would post the same answer twice. Use `gh` to comment only where the task asks for it, such as on another issue.

